winemac.drv: Give a child of another process's window its own Mac window.

A window that is SetParent'ed into a window of another process (Adobe AIR's
ApolloRuntimeContentWindow inside a launcher's main window) can't draw into its
parent's surface, and winemac destroyed its Mac window, so nothing showed.

Such a window now keeps its own window surface (win32u) and a borderless Mac
window (winemac), placed at its screen position and ordered just above the Mac
window of its top-level ancestor. The parent process publishes that Mac window's
number in a window property and posts WM_MACDRV_SYNC_FOREIGN_CHILD to foreign
descendants when it moves, is reordered or hidden, or when its app is activated.

GL/Vulkan client surfaces (wined3d, so also D3D9 in AIR) and mouse capture use
get_cocoa_toplevel() instead of GA_ROOT: the topmost ancestor in this process,
which owns the Mac window. With GA_ROOT the client view was never attached and
the child stayed white.

A window can also have several GL drawables: win32u makes one per context made
current on it, and wined3d in AIR makes two. Each new drawable showed its own
view and hid the previous one, so frames swapped into the first went to a
hidden view and the window stayed white. The drawable that swaps now shows its
view (macdrv_client_surface_show). This part also affects ordinary windows.

Mouse input is sent naming the window that owns the Mac window
(get_cocoa_toplevel) instead of GA_ROOT. The server makes the named window the
cursor window and asks its thread to set the cursor; with GA_ROOT that was the
other process, which set its own cursor (often none), so the pointer vanished
over the child.

A process whose Mac windows are all such children (AIR's lobby process) becomes
an accessory app instead of a regular one, so it adds no Dock icon or menu bar
but can still be activated and take keyboard focus. Showing an ordinary window
later still makes it a regular app, and it goes back to being an accessory app
once only such children are shown. AIR's preloader shows an ordinary window
before its lobby window is reparented, and keeps a zero-size window, which
doesn't make an accessory app regular. A regular process that is left with no
window shown at all (a launcher that hides itself after starting the game, or
explorer.exe) becomes an accessory app too, after 2 seconds so that going from
one window to the next doesn't make its Dock icon flicker.

Test: scripts/test-xproc-child.sh (demo/xprocchild.c).

